CAT = "rscookSafety"; SEG = "SAF"
Q = [
("Which extinguisher class is made for fires in commercial deep fryers?",
 "Class K", ["Class B", "Class C", "Class D"],
 "Class K agents saponify hot cooking oil and cool it, which is what a fryer fire needs. Class B covers flammable liquids in general but is not rated for deep commercial fryer fires."),
("Oil in a sauté pan catches fire on the range. What should you do first?",
 "Cover it with a metal lid", ["Carry it to the dish sink", "Spray it with a water mist", "Pour flour over the flames"],
 "Covering the pan cuts off oxygen, then turn off the heat. Water makes burning oil spatter and spread, carrying the pan spreads the fire, and flour can ignite."),
("A sanitizer is poured from its jug into a spray bottle. Under WHMIS, what must the bottle carry?",
 "A workplace label", ["A supplier batch number", "A copy of the invoice", "A hazard symbol drawn by hand"],
 "WHMIS requires a workplace label on a container a product is decanted into, naming the product and how to handle it safely, unless it is used up right away. A hand-drawn symbol alone does not meet the rule."),
("A chef's knife slips off the cutting board. What should you do?",
 "Step back and let it fall", ["Catch it by the handle", "Trap it against the counter", "Block it with your foot"],
 "A falling knife is never caught or blocked. Step back, let it land, then pick it up by the handle."),
("Which glove belongs on the guiding hand when using a mandoline?",
 "Cut-resistant glove", ["Nitrile disposable glove", "Heat-resistant mitt", "Latex disposable glove"],
 "A cut-resistant glove protects the hand that feeds the food past the blade. Disposable gloves keep food clean but stop no cuts, and a mitt guards against heat."),
("Why is nail polish discouraged for cooks handling food?",
 "Flakes can fall into food", ["It hides cuts under the nail", "Heat makes it give off fumes", "It reacts with sanitizers"],
 "Chipped polish is a physical contaminant that can end up in food. The RSOS lists nail polish with fragrances and jewellery as personal items to manage."),
("Health Canada gives which range as the temperature danger zone?",
 "4°C to 60°C", ["0°C to 60°C", "4°C to 74°C", "10°C to 54°C"],
 "Health Canada sets the danger zone between 4°C and 60°C, where bacteria grow quickly. 74°C is the reheating and poultry-piece target, not the top of the zone."),
("A walk-in cooler should hold food at or below which temperature?",
 "4°C", ["5°C", "7°C", "10°C"],
 "Health Canada advises refrigerators at 4°C or lower, the bottom edge of the danger zone. 5°C is the figure used in some other countries."),
("What does an infrared thermometer measure on food?",
 "Surface temperature only", ["Internal temperature", "Core and surface together", "Air temperature in the unit"],
 "An infrared (laser) thermometer reads the surface it is pointed at. Internal temperatures need a probe thermometer pushed into the thickest part."),
("Leftover soup is reheated for service. What internal temperature must it reach?",
 "74°C", ["60°C", "63°C", "71°C"],
 "Leftovers are reheated to 74°C, and soups, sauces and gravies brought to a full boil. 60°C is only the hot-holding line and 71°C is the ground-meat target."),
("Cooked rice sat on the counter for three hours. What should be done with it?",
 "Discard it", ["Reheat it to 74°C", "Chill it in a blast chiller", "Serve it within the hour"],
 "Health Canada advises throwing out cooked food left at room temperature for more than two hours. Reheating does not destroy toxins some bacteria leave behind."),
("A large pot of chili must be cooled fast. What is the best first step?",
 "Divide it into shallow pans", ["Place the full pot in the walk-in", "Cover it tightly while it cools", "Leave it on a cold burner"],
 "Shallow, smaller batches lose heat quickly, and ice wands or an ice bath speed it further. A full pot in a walk-in stays warm in the centre for hours and warms nearby food."),
("Why is raw meat kept on the bottom shelf of a cooler?",
 "Its juices can't drip on other food", ["It needs the coldest air in the unit", "It is used before other food", "Its weight keeps shelves stable"],
 "Storing raw meat, poultry and fish below ready-to-eat food stops raw juices dripping onto it. The point is cross-contamination, not the coldest air."),
("A probe thermometer placed in an ice slurry should read:",
 "0°C", ["-2°C", "1°C", "4°C"],
 "An ice-water slurry sits at 0°C, so a calibrated thermometer reads 0°C in it. A reading of 4°C would mean the probe needs adjusting."),
("In a three-compartment sink, what is the order of steps?",
 "Wash, rinse, sanitize", ["Rinse, wash, sanitize", "Wash, sanitize, rinse", "Sanitize, wash, rinse"],
 "Detergent removes soil, the rinse removes detergent that would weaken the sanitizer, and the sanitizer then kills what remains. Items then air-dry."),
("How should pans leave the sanitizing sink?",
 "Air-dried on a rack", ["Towel-dried by hand", "Rinsed under clean water", "Stacked while still wet"],
 "Air-drying keeps the sanitized surface from being recontaminated by a cloth. Rinsing after a no-rinse sanitizer removes its effect, and wet stacking traps moisture."),
("What do test strips measure in a sanitizer bucket?",
 "Sanitizer concentration", ["Water hardness", "Detergent residue", "Water temperature"],
 "Test strips confirm the sanitizer is at the strength on the label, which is logged. Too weak fails to sanitize; too strong can leave a chemical residue."),
("Which way of thawing a raw turkey is unsafe?",
 "On the counter overnight", ["In the walk-in cooler", "Under cold running water", "In a sink of cold water, changed often"],
 "Thawing at room temperature lets the outer layers sit in the danger zone for hours. Refrigerator thawing and cold water thawing keep the surface cold."),
("Norovirus usually reaches food in a kitchen through:",
 "Sick workers' hands", ["Undercooked poultry", "Rice left warm", "Dented cans"],
 "Norovirus is carried by sick people and spreads from hands to ready-to-eat food. Salmonella is linked to poultry, Bacillus cereus to warm rice and botulism to damaged cans."),
("How should a cook lift a heavy stock pot from the floor?",
 "Bend the knees, back straight", ["Bend at the waist, legs straight", "Twist toward the stove while lifting", "Lift with the arms held out"],
 "Bending the knees and keeping the load close lets the legs do the work. Bending at the waist or twisting under load strains the lower back."),
]
